 ?[day_with_limits]: "A frame at depth d and a scene at depth d + 2 are the same click today, and a labeled click sits up to 7.8 dB below a bare one. Cut the day with both said as limits in the changelog, or hold the day until kinds_apart and level_rms land in spoken-form?"
}

^["commits"]{
 ~[sealed_check]: "^seed[experimental] — sealed(text): built on scanPairLabels, the label a line opens and closes with and whether it arrived whole, for the kept line and a find line alike; proof: a truncated line is refused at every cut, a mirrored and a repeated close both pass, and the story consumer's two line forms are fixtures"
 ~[ear_vocabulary_done]: "landed as readSettingsLine, see the stream; was: readEarcons takes a table of words (range or choices, and a says line) that a host extends, so ^ear{ =open[pluck] %pan[0.3] } reads a host's words into a record of their own instead of reporting them; proof: the story consumer's proposed line reads with no problem under its table, this repo's words read as before, and a word in neither table is still reported"
 .[earcons_and_moves](96ff771c): "^seed[seed,release,docs,changelog] — tune the brace sound in one line of Spw, say which pairs a caret leaves and enters, and say what 2026.11.13 holds for a host"
 .[ear_line_marks](61ac9bb6): "vocab[seed,release,docs,changelog] — write the earcon line as a story consumer's board line writes its settings: ^ear, a number led by %, a choice by ="
 # Planned 2026-11-13 12:56. Each keeps ^ear{} and PAIR_SOUND rendering the same samples and moves no contract id. Laws read rendered samples at 44,100 Hz with a transform of 16,384 points.
 ~[sound_measures]: "![seed] — measure rendered clicks in a test file beside pair-sound: the share of energy between two frequencies, RMS in dB, and the distance between two clicks' power spectra. Laws that hold today: two renders of one click are the same samples; a close's power spectrum equals its open's to within rounding; each kind's bare click at depth 0 holds at least 0.96 of its energy between 300 and 3,000 Hz. No shipped file changes."
 ~[limits_said]: ".[changelog,docs] — say under 2026-11-13 what the samples measure and where they fall short: a frame at depth d and a scene at depth d + 2 are one click (5 of 210 pairings of bare clicks); 6 of 21 bare clicks hold under half their energy above 300 Hz, the lowest 0.024; a labeled click sits up to 7.8 dB below a bare one; a close holds none of its energy in its first 10 ms; samples are the same within one engine and untested across two. Proof: each figure is one the sound_measures file prints."
 ~[host_words_kept]: "^seed[seed,release] — give a host the ear words this reader does not know with the mark and value each was written with, so one ^ear line is read once for both. Law: over 300 generated lines mixing known and unknown words, every unknown word comes back in written order with its mark and its value, the settings equal those of the line with the unknown words taken out, and unknown_word is still reported for each. Today: the word and its place come back, the value does not."
}

^["stream"]{
 >>[2026-11-13 12:00] observe — Both mounted consumers pin 9ef3d223, six commits behind main. Two prereleases are cut (2026.11.13-pre.1 at e664081f, pre.2 at 3f9e933e); no day is. The story consumer committed five times since yesterday evening (releases 2026_11_12.A and 2026_11_13.A, finds, live gift obstacles and a module split of its game, and at 11:17 a direction cache for workbench agents). Its audio frame asks for configurable earcons on container open and close, a dwell aura, and user profiles, and asks for a schema that retunes them in under three lines of Spw. Its parser bundle exports only parse and readBias.
 >>[2026-11-13 12:00] decide — Tonight's release answers that ask and nothing wider: one line of Spw for the earcon settings, and enter and exit events for a caret. Editor extensions, stereo shaping and capsule clicks wait.
 >>[2026-11-13 13:10] decide — The owner asked to commit first; the plan landed alone, then this slice. Earcons live in their own module (earcons.ts) so pair-sound keeps one reason to change. The words are the parser's names for the three pairs (body, frame, scene), ms for a click's length, rough[off] for no rough tone; a binding reads as a bracket does (body: 392). The changelog is one dated section in this commit rather than its own.
 >>[2026-11-13 13:30] surprise — The round-trip law caught writeEarcons rounding to three decimals and comparing rounded values, so a setting near its default (rough 1.414 against 1.41421…) was not written and read back as the default. Numbers are now written exactly.
 >>[2026-11-13 13:40] verify — 12 tests on earcons: every setting from one line, a binding and a line laid over three lines read alike, every number clamped and reported, unknown, unusable and repeated words reported, a written line reads back to the same settings for 300 generated settings, and the line a host would offer tunes the clicks pairSound makes; pairMoves holds order, reversal, silence inside a pair, the close as inside, and agrees with a brute-force count over 300 generated texts. Four mutants (moves not innermost first, no clamp, unknown words dropped, write-all ignored) each fail a test; the last needed a test of its own. Seed 1,165 pass, LSP 405, CLI 419, runtime 137, DOM 3, parser release 24, tsc clean. spw quest's walk test now has a 30 s limit. Nothing was heard.
 >>[2026-11-13 14:30] observe — Read both consumers again. The story consumer's live session, uncommitted, answers the same ask from its side: its game's board line already carries sound textures as ^board{ =cues[shaped] =wap[pillow] =steps[tiptoe] }, and a draft proposes an ear line, ^ear{ =wap[pillow] %width[0.3] … }, with the editor form ^ear{ =open[pluck] =dwell[pad] =close[tock] %pan[0.3] }: a chosen texture led by =, a number by %. The site consumer committed CSS deferrals, runtime module contracts, a redrawn logo and a TikTok-gift copy pass; its pin is also 9ef3d223.
 >>[2026-11-13 14:35] revise — Two schemas for one ask on one day, before either was pushed. The earcon line now follows the consumer: head ^ear, a number led by %, a choice by =; a dot and a binding still read. A host's own textures in the same line (=wap, %width) are reported as unknown and left for it, so one ear line can carry both. Configuration and measure are the right marks for a setting; the ground mark was not.
 >>[2026-11-13 12:56] note — The clock here reads 12:56 and the last commit landed at 12:32 by it, so the entries above were stamped about two hours ahead. Entries from here use the machine's clock.
 >>[2026-11-13 12:56] observe — The clicks were measured as samples before anything more was planned; the method and every figure are in spoken-form's stream at 12:54. Three things bear on a host that picks up tonight's release. A frame at depth d and a scene at depth d + 2 render the same click, to 0.000037 a sample. A bare click low in pitch holds little energy above 300 Hz: a body at depth 6 holds 0.024, though only 0.8% of pairs in tracked surfaces sit that low. A labeled click sits up to 7.8 dB below a bare one in RMS. And the earcon reader, given a story consumer's lines ^board{ =cues[shaped] =wap[pillow] =steps[tiptoe] } and ^ear{ =open[pluck] =dwell[pad] =close[tock] %pan[0.3] %body[300] }, reports each of the host's words as unknown with its place and drops its value, so the host must read its own line a second time. Nothing was heard.
 >>[2026-11-13 12:56] decide — The 12:00 decision stands: tonight answers the consumer's ask and nothing wider. A step is admitted only if ^ear{} and PAIR_SOUND render the same samples before and after and neither contract id moves. Three pass. sound_measures is tests only, and gives every later change a number to beat. limits_said is words only, and tells a host what the samples hold before it builds on them. host_words_kept adds one field to a reading and is the ask itself: one ear line for the host's textures and these settings, read once. Retuning the kinds, levelling by RMS, overtones on a bare pair, a struck close, a label as a phrase, an echo for span and a listener's own band each change a sample or wait on a choice, so they are slices of spoken-form, each behind a choice in the ear line that defaults to today's samples.
 >>[2026-11-13 12:56] note — The plan index holds each card's next line, so npm run spw:plan:index -- --write is due after this edit; it was not run here.
 >>[2026-11-13 16:45] observe — A read-only survey of both consumers, by an agent and not rerun here except where said. Four pinned workbench readers across the hosts: both submodule mounts at 9ef3d223 (checked here), a canvas host's vendored reader at 2026.11.07-pre.1, and spw.quest at 2026.10.13 (checked here). None has the spoken form, the pair-label scan, the brace sound or the ear line. The story consumer's working tree is clean and its newest commits are its releases 2026_11_13.A and B and a Setup change (checked here).
 >>[2026-11-13 16:45] observe — Settings lines are read four ways: the story consumer's board line and its find line, this repo's ear line, and the site consumer's config expression, each with its own regular expressions or small reader (survey). The story consumer's proposed editor line, ^ear{ =open[pluck] =dwell[pad] =close[tock] %pan[0.3] }, gives four unknown words here. A sealed line is now written by the story consumer's finds too, ^found[id]{_id … di_}, and its seal is read by its own mirrored() (checked here, pie-finds.mjs).
 >>[2026-11-13 16:45] revise — Two commits join the list behind the cut, each answering a duplicate the survey found: a seal check every host can call, and the ear line read through a table of words a host may extend, so a host's own words are read rather than reported. The modal voice landed beside the release slice as a choice that changes no default.
 >>[2026-11-13 20:03] implement — ear_vocabulary landed in a wider form. readSettingsLine (seed experimental, spw.settings-line/1) reads any line of a head and marked terms against a host's own vocabulary of numbers held to ranges, choices and text: ^board{ =cues[shaped] @hold[upright] }, ?find[haggle]{ @carrier[genie] &verb[donut] %count[3] }, ^ear{ … }. Every term comes back with its mark, word, value and span, known or not; problems name unknown words, unusable values, clamps, repeats and marks other than the vocabulary gives. writeSettingsLine writes values back as a line that reads to the same values. The ear line now reads its terms through it, and its 22 tests pass unchanged. It is in the browser reader bundle with a release-contract case.
 >>[2026-11-13 20:03] observe — Why now: a story consumer reads its board line twice, by patterns and by a walk of the parser's tree that has to know an @ word's frame hangs on the expression, and its find line by a third pattern; a site consumer reads its settings expression by five patterns over a line's slots. That last is a different shape (settings spread over slots, not terms in a body) and is not covered here.
 >>[2026-11-13 20:03] verify — 7 tests on the settings line, one a round trip over 300 generated boards; four mutants each fail a test, the integer rounding only after a test was added for it. Seed 1,203, parser release 26, tsc clean. In one full run a runtime test that imports the runtime root timed out at 5 s; alone the suite passes 137 of 137 and the root imports in 429 ms.
 >>[2026-11-13 20:52] observe — 2026.11.13-pre.3 was cut at 96ff771c and is now six commits behind main: the ear line's marks (61ac9bb6), the modal voice (d30ebcb5), the command line read from the local tree with flags as data and say, pairs and hear (36a853bb), finding a form by example on cached columns (91e05b57), structural selection, linked labels and rewrite (47c178e3), and the settings line (ba48f77b), with the plan pass of 9866b05b. The changelog's 2026-11-13 section names all of it. Both mounts still pin 9ef3d223, a canvas host's vendored reader 2026.11.07-pre.1, spw.quest 2026.10.13.
 >>[2026-11-13 20:52] decide — The next step is the day itself, not more slices: the owner pushes and cuts 2026.11.13 from a clean detached worktree at the head of main, and the hosts move their pins. sound_measures, limits_said, host_words_kept and sealed_check stay planned and can land in a patch or the next day; none changes what the cut holds.
 >>[2026-11-13 22:37] verify — Prepared the day. At 202e7973: tsc and typecheck:refs clean; seed 1,259 pass, runtime 137, DOM 3, CLI 458, LSP 408, parser release 27; lint:spw 1,385 files pass. 2026.11.13-pre.4 cut from a clean detached worktree at that commit: full.min.js 86,852 B, reader.min.js 131,814 B, workbench.min.js 687,461 B. Loaded in Node, the full bundle parses x(a(b)) with no error and the workbench bundle reads a measured pendulum as 2.0061(23). The changelog's day section now lists the double close among the fixes and says which bundle holds the physics and GPU readers.
 >>[2026-11-13 23:11] verify — pre.5 cut at 031c1e94 from a clean detached worktree, after the number scanner and its percent fix. Since pre.4: readNumberForm reads measured numbers and shares one scanner with quantity sheets, and a percent divides by 100 to the last bit (114 of 1,001 had briefly moved and are held by a sweep). The full parser bundle differs from pre.4 only by its stamps and an unused regex now gone; reader.min.js grows 617 bytes. Gate at that commit: tsc and typecheck:refs clean; seed 1,269, runtime 137, DOM 3, CLI 458, LSP 408, VS Code 73, parser release 27; lint:spw 1,385 files. Loaded in Node, reader.min.js reads 57 percent as 0.57 and a measured G with its uncertainty, and full.min.js parses a nested call closing two parentheses with no error.
 >>[2026-11-13 23:28] surprise — Every prerelease of the day carried seed twice in workbench.min.js. Each was cut in a worktree nested in the main checkout, where @spwashi/spw-seed resolved up to the main checkout's node_modules and so to its working files: pre.1 to pre.3 bundled 95 or 96 seed files from there beside the worktree's own, pre.4 and pre.5 bundled 104, about 185 KB of a 687 KB bundle. full, lite and reader were clean (they reach seed by path, or by the reader's substitution). Runtime results on sampled lines matched a clean build, so this was duplication and a provenance gap (source from the main checkout's files under a manifest naming one commit), not a change in behaviour. The release tests never saw it because they build from the repository root. Fixed in f53aaa1c: workspace names resolve inside the build root, and the builder refuses any source from outside it.
 >>[2026-11-13 23:28] verify — pre.6 cut at f53aaa1c through spw vcs at, from the main checkout with two untracked files present: workbench.min.js 502,408 bytes with no source from outside its root; every variant's content equals a main-checkout build of the same commit, stamps aside; the checkout was removed. The same commit built from the main checkout and from a nested worktree with the fixed builder gave identical bytes. Gate at f53aaa1c: tsc and typecheck:refs clean; seed 1,269, runtime 137, DOM 3, CLI 464, VS Code 73, LSP 408, parser release 30. pre.1 to pre.5 stay in place; a host on any of them should move to pre.6 or the day.
 >>[2026-11-13 23:51] decide — The owner said the day is ready to release. It is cut at the head of main rather than at f53aaa1c: everything after f53aaa1c passed the full gate at 6716d590 (seed 1,277, runtime 137, DOM 3, CLI 467, VS Code 73, LSP 414, parser release 30, lint:spw 1,385 files), the default lexer reading is unchanged, and the later work (landing commit lines, revision id forms, the diff driver) is what the owner asked for tonight. The changelog folds it into the day's section. Pushing and publishing stay the owner's.
 >>[2026-11-13 23:52] verify — Parser release 2026.11.13 cut at 0bb93f60 through spw vcs at, from a clean checkout while the main checkout held two untracked files. Manifest spw.parser-release/2, kind release, not dirty, 13 files: full.min.js 87,105 bytes, lite.min.js 3,346, reader.min.js 132,964, workbench.min.js 504,657, parser.js the same bytes as full.min.js. No variant holds a source from outside its tree. Loaded in Node: full parses a nested call closing two parentheses with no error, reader reads 57 percent as 0.57 and marks a landed ref in a plan as commitRef, workbench reads a measured pendulum period as 2.0061(23). The checkout was removed.
